{% extends layout %}
{% block page_body %}
	{% set ph_kicker = 'Support' %}
	{% set ph_title = 'Help' %}
	{% set ph_lead = 'How the platform works, and where to get answers.' %}
	{% include '@platform/partials/page-head.html.twig' %}
	<section class="container cl-medium py-5">
		<div class="cl-help-accordion" data-help-accordion>
			<details class="cl-help-group" open>
				<summary>
					<span class="cl-help-group-title">Getting around</span>
					<span class="cl-help-group-hint">Where things are, and what each area is for</span>
				</summary>
				<div class="cl-help-group-body">
					<details class="cl-help-item">
						<summary>
							The main areas
						</summary>
						<div class="cl-help-body">
							<p>
								<strong>My Course Library</strong> holds the courses you are taking, have completed, have marked as favourites or have requested.
							</p>
							<p>
								<strong>Profile</strong> contains your personal particulars, email addresses and active sessions.
							</p>
							<p>
								<strong>Administration</strong> is where platform administrators manage courses, people, companies, enrolments, credits, activity, reports, themes and settings.
							</p>
						</div>
					</details>
					<details class="cl-help-item">
						<summary>
							Signing in
						</summary>
						<div class="cl-help-body">
							<p>
								There is no password. You give an email address, the platform sends a single-use link, and opening it signs you in on that device.
							</p>
							<p>
								A link is good once and expires. Requesting a new one invalidates the last, so the most recent email is always the one to use.
							</p>
						</div>
					</details>
					<details class="cl-help-item">
						<summary>
							Real and generated data
						</summary>
						<div class="cl-help-body">
							<p>
								Administrators can see two separate populations: the genuine one, and generated sets used to exercise the platform. The switch above a list says which you are looking at, and it appears only where you may choose.
							</p>
							<p>
								Nothing crosses between them. A generated learner cannot be enrolled on a genuine course, and the database refuses it independently of the application.
							</p>
						</div>
					</details>
				</div>
			</details>
			<details class="cl-help-group">
				<summary>
					<span class="cl-help-group-title">People, companies and access</span>
					<span class="cl-help-group-hint">Who may do what, and on whose behalf</span>
				</summary>
				<div class="cl-help-group-body">
					<details class="cl-help-item">
						<summary>
							Roles
						</summary>
						<div class="cl-help-body">
							<p>
								Platform Administrators can open any person and edit their complete profile, company and roles. Course Owners control the courses they own; Course Editors edit the courses assigned to them; Learners take courses.
							</p>
							<p>
								A role is a bundle of permissions, and a permission is what is actually checked. Changing a role takes effect on the next request without anyone signing out.
							</p>
						</div>
					</details>
					<details class="cl-help-item">
						<summary>
							Companies
						</summary>
						<div class="cl-help-body">
							<p>
								The permanent System Company represents the organisation hosting the platform. People who do not belong to a third-party company belong to it.
							</p>
							<p>
								A company reserves one email domain. Its administrator and learners use addresses at that exact domain.
							</p>
						</div>
					</details>
					<details class="cl-help-item">
						<summary>
							Course credits
						</summary>
						<div class="cl-help-body">
							<p>
								A company buys credits against a course and assigns them to its people. A credit is consumed when the learner starts the course, not when it is assigned, so an unstarted assignment can still be withdrawn and given to someone else.
							</p>
							<p>
								Once a course is started the credit is spent and cannot be returned.
							</p>
						</div>
					</details>
				</div>
			</details>
			<details class="cl-help-group">
				<summary>
					<span class="cl-help-group-title">Courses</span>
					<span class="cl-help-group-hint">Authoring, publishing and what learners see</span>
				</summary>
				<div class="cl-help-group-body">
					<details class="cl-help-item">
						<summary>
							Publishing
						</summary>
						<div class="cl-help-body">
							<p>
								A course must carry at least one price option before it can be published. Publishing stamps the date once: unpublishing and republishing does not move it.
							</p>
							<p>
								A published course stays editable in place. Editing it does not create a second course, and learners already on it keep their progress.
							</p>
						</div>
					</details>
					<details class="cl-help-item">
						<summary>
							Categories and tags
						</summary>
						<div class="cl-help-body">
							<p>
								A course sits in one category, three levels deep at most. Tags are cross-cutting labels and a course may carry as many as it needs.
							</p>
							<p>
								Both are shared vocabulary rather than per-population data, so a generated course and a genuine one can sit under the same category. What is counted under a label is still filtered to the population you are looking at.
							</p>
						</div>
					</details>
					<details class="cl-help-item">
						<summary>
							Reset and deletion
						</summary>
						<div class="cl-help-body">
							<p>
								<strong>Reset</strong> removes course content and learner test data but keeps the course shell for re-import.
							</p>
							<p>
								<strong>Delete</strong> permanently removes the course together with the access, progress, results and certificates that belong to it.
							</p>
						</div>
					</details>
					<details class="cl-help-item">
						<summary>
							Assessments and diagnostics
						</summary>
						<div class="cl-help-body">
							<p>
								A module assessment is taken after its module; the final assessment unlocks when the required modules are assessed. Diagnostics sit before Module 1 and are recorded separately from module and final grades.
							</p>
							<p>
								A diagnostic can be configured to complete the course when its threshold is met, which is how a course allows a learner to prove they already know the material.
							</p>
						</div>
					</details>
				</div>
			</details>
			<details class="cl-help-group">
				<summary>
					<span class="cl-help-group-title">Themes</span>
					<span class="cl-help-group-hint">Installing a release, and writing one</span>
				</summary>
				<div class="cl-help-group-body">
					<details class="cl-help-item" id="theme-sdk">
						<summary>
							Installing and switching
						</summary>
						<div class="cl-help-body">
							<p>
								Themes are Theme Package 4.0 ZIP releases described by <span class="mono">theme.json</span>. Administration can validate and install a release, preview it, activate it, or uninstall it while it is inactive.
							</p>
							<p>
								Theme source is not edited or exported by the platform. A child theme names one exact parent by name and version, and supplies at least its own <span class="mono">public/css/theme.css</span>.
							</p>
						</div>
					</details>
					<details class="cl-help-item">
						<summary>
							Writing a theme
						</summary>
						<div class="cl-help-body">
							<p>
								A minimal standalone theme needs a manifest, <span class="mono">base.html.twig</span> and a non-empty <span class="mono">public/css/theme.css</span>. A full theme can add page-family wrappers, partials, browser JavaScript, assets and declared HTTPS CDN resources.
							</p>
							<p>
								The base declares where the page renders; a page-family wrapper extends the base and wraps that same block. A theme supplies presentation only - it never executes server code and never replaces the platform's security or business rules.
							</p>
							<p>
								Developer documentation is in <span class="mono">docs/THEME-SDK.md</span>, including the navigation and component styling contracts a theme has to honour.
							</p>
						</div>
					</details>
				</div>
			</details>
		</div>
	</section>
{% endblock %}
